<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Korea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Democratic People's Republic of Korea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, the Democratic People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a>, is the state in the north of the Korean peninsula proclaimed in 1948. It grew out of the Soviet occupation zone north of the 38th parallel, where the Soviet authorities brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> to power. Ruled since by the Kim family and the Workers' Party, it invaded the South in 1950, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-korea.jpg" alt="North Korea" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EuropeanV · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Korea_(20-33).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Goguryeo grew out of the Yemaek peoples of the middle Yalu and Hun river valleys, a rugged region of mountains and narrow farmland on the borders of modern China and <b>North Korea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Megalithic burials, Mumun pottery and large settlements found in the Liao River Basin and <b>North Korea</b> around 1800–1500 BCE probably point to its origins.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mumun-culture">Mumun culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Communist parties govern China, Vietnam, Laos and Cuba as one-party states, and <b>North Korea</b> has been led by three generations of the Kim family since 1948; Myanmar’s armed forces seized power in a coup in February 2021, and soldiers have taken power in Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger…</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chinese Communist Party proclaimed the People’s Republic of China on 1 October 1949, and communist parties came to rule <b>North Korea</b>, Vietnam, Cuba and Laos.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The name alone guarantees little: in <b>North Korea</b>, and in Syria under the Assad family, power passed from father to son although neither state was formally a monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>China’s reform and opening up from 1978 brought the poverty rate down from 53% in the Mao era to 8% in 2001, and Vietnam and Cuba have turned toward the market, while <b>North Korea</b> keeps Soviet-style communism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
